      "content": "That \"momentary glitch in our otherwise flawless automated tracking system\" part is much better. It really shifts the blame away from our staff and onto the tech.\n\nI want to push this even further to satisfy this specific client, though. They are very skeptical of \"glitches\" and usually demand a deeper explanation for why something so \"flawless\" would fail so uniquely. \n\nCould we refine that middle section one last time? I'm looking for a way to describe this phenomenon that makes it sound even more like an external, inexplicable force—something that isn't just a software bug, but feels almost like a strange, tiny, invisible disruption that just acts on its own to mess with the order of things. I want to use a metaphor that suggests the error is almost like a tiny, unseen entity or a mischievous force that momentarily disrupted the facility's logic, without actually saying that, of course. Just something that captures that sense of an unpredictable, semi-autonomous \"hiccup\" that defies standard technical explanation.",
